1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,943
This is Capybara Onsen.

2
00:00:01,943 --> 00:00:04,920
More and more of its guests are not people.

3
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:09,075
They are AI agents, booking a soak on someone else's behalf.

4
00:00:09,975 --> 00:00:14,621
The onsen is about to ship an accessibility upgrade to its booking form.

5
00:00:14,621 --> 00:00:17,976
To a person, the new version looks exactly the same.

6
00:00:17,976 --> 00:00:19,719
Not one pixel is different.

7
00:00:20,619 --> 00:00:23,241
But an AI agent does not look at pixels.

8
00:00:23,241 --> 00:00:25,339
It reads the accessibility tree.

9
00:00:25,339 --> 00:00:29,207
And there, the two versions could hardly be more different:

10
00:00:29,207 --> 00:00:33,993
unnamed controls on one side, buttons with names and states on the other.

11
00:00:34,893 --> 00:00:38,716
So before the upgrade goes to everyone, one question.

12
00:00:38,716 --> 00:00:41,096
What does it do to the AI guests?

13
00:00:41,096 --> 00:00:42,755
We expected it to help.

14
00:00:42,755 --> 00:00:46,361
Instead of arguing about it, we ran an experiment.

15
00:00:47,261 --> 00:00:51,032
Both versions sit behind one Flagsmith feature flag,

16
00:00:51,032 --> 00:00:54,078
on a Flagsmith instance we host ourselves.

17
00:00:54,078 --> 00:00:59,735
A Flagsmith experiment splits the guests fifty-fifty and watches four metrics:

18
00:00:59,735 --> 00:01:03,797
correct bookings, completed bookings, steps, and tokens.

19
00:01:04,697 --> 00:01:08,338
The guests are language models driving real browsers.

20
00:01:08,338 --> 00:01:13,765
Each one gets a booking to make, and no hint about which version it was served.

21
00:01:13,765 --> 00:01:16,169
Watch this one on the classic form.

22
00:01:16,169 --> 00:01:20,703
Its preferred time is sold out, but nothing in the markup says so.

23
00:01:20,703 --> 00:01:24,001
It clicks, nothing happens, and it clicks again.

24
00:01:24,901 --> 00:01:28,935
On the accessible form, the same time is marked as disabled.

25
00:01:28,935 --> 00:01:33,103
The guest reads that, and goes straight to the next free slot.

26
00:01:34,003 --> 00:01:36,812
The experiment itself is run by a coding agent,

27
00:01:36,812 --> 00:01:39,980
using a skill we built on the Flagsmith command line.

28
00:01:39,980 --> 00:01:44,104
Before anything goes live, it checks that every event really arrives,

29
00:01:44,104 --> 00:01:46,673
and that every user still gets the control.

30
00:01:46,673 --> 00:01:50,199
Then it stops, and waits for a person to approve the start.

31
00:01:51,099 --> 00:01:54,443
Three hundred guests later, this is Flagsmith's analysis.

32
00:01:55,343 --> 00:01:59,539
Correct bookings: 156 out of 156 on the classic form,

33
00:01:59,539 --> 00:02:02,785
and 144 out of 144 on the accessible one.

34
00:02:02,785 --> 00:02:04,448
No difference at all.

35
00:02:04,448 --> 00:02:06,427
Steps went down a little.

36
00:02:06,427 --> 00:02:10,861
But tokens per guest went up by almost fourteen percent,

37
00:02:10,861 --> 00:02:13,315
and that guardrail is breached.

38
00:02:14,215 --> 00:02:14,533
Why?

39
00:02:14,533 --> 00:02:17,949
A capable agent recovers from its mistakes,

40
00:02:17,949 --> 00:02:21,285
so the bookings come out right either way.

41
00:02:21,285 --> 00:02:25,654
But on the classic form, 24 guests took a detour first.

42
00:02:25,654 --> 00:02:28,275
On the accessible form, none did.

43
00:02:28,275 --> 00:02:33,915
And yet the accessible page takes longer to read, on every single step.

44
00:02:33,915 --> 00:02:36,218
Fewer mistakes, more reading.

45
00:02:36,218 --> 00:02:37,569
The reading wins.

46
00:02:38,469 --> 00:02:41,791
So when the agent tries to ship, the skill refuses.

47
00:02:41,791 --> 00:02:44,136
A breached guardrail needs a person.

48
00:02:44,136 --> 00:02:47,523
A person was shown the result and accepted the cost:

49
00:02:47,523 --> 00:02:51,170
the upgrade is for people, and agents book just as well.

50
00:02:51,170 --> 00:02:53,515
Flagsmith rolled it out to everyone.

51
00:02:54,415 --> 00:02:56,343
And you can check it yourself.

52
00:02:56,343 --> 00:03:00,135
On a fresh clone, one command starts open-source Flagsmith,

53
00:03:00,135 --> 00:03:03,606
the booking site, and a hundred and twenty new guests.

54
00:03:03,606 --> 00:03:06,562
Seven minutes later, it gives the same answer.

55
00:03:07,462 --> 00:03:11,772
One experiment, an answer we did not expect, and a result nobody scripted.
